1
00:00:00,370 --> 00:00:01,982
Uncountable nouns.

2
00:00:02,022 --> 00:00:03,842
Why can’t we
add s to them?

3
00:00:03,882 --> 00:00:04,920
And why can’t
they take

4
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:06,384
a plural verb?

5
00:00:07,337 --> 00:00:09,267
Countable
nouns are easy:

6
00:00:09,307 --> 00:00:10,948
a chair,
two chairs,

7
00:00:10,988 --> 00:00:12,225
three ideas.

8
00:00:12,265 --> 00:00:13,563
We count them
one by one,

9
00:00:13,603 --> 00:00:15,295
so they
can take s.

10
00:00:15,937 --> 00:00:17,192
But what
about water,

11
00:00:17,232 --> 00:00:19,529
rice, honesty,
or music?

12
00:00:19,569 --> 00:00:21,181
Nobody says
two waters,

13
00:00:21,221 --> 00:00:22,791
or three
honesties.

14
00:00:23,537 --> 00:00:24,849
Most books
just give you

15
00:00:24,889 --> 00:00:26,323
a list
to memorise.

16
00:00:26,363 --> 00:00:28,717
We won’t.
We’ll use logic.

17
00:00:28,757 --> 00:00:30,632
Uncountable
nouns fall into

18
00:00:30,672 --> 00:00:31,972
five categories,

19
00:00:32,012 --> 00:00:34,735
and each category
has its own reason.

20
00:00:35,370 --> 00:00:37,836
One: unit,
or mass.

21
00:00:37,876 --> 00:00:38,968
Two: material.

22
00:00:39,008 --> 00:00:40,225
Three: measured.

23
00:00:40,265 --> 00:00:41,110
Four: abstract.

24
00:00:41,150 --> 00:00:41,603
And five:

25
00:00:41,643 --> 00:00:43,112
activity,
or process.

26
00:00:43,837 --> 00:00:46,515
Let’s prove them
one at a time.

27
00:00:47,637 --> 00:00:48,484
Error one.

28
00:00:48,524 --> 00:00:50,230
‘Furniture
are expensive.’

29
00:00:50,270 --> 00:00:51,277
That’s wrong.

30
00:00:51,317 --> 00:00:52,738
The correct
sentence is:

31
00:00:52,778 --> 00:00:54,537
‘Furniture
is expensive.’

32
00:00:54,577 --> 00:00:55,571
But why?

33
00:00:56,303 --> 00:00:57,438
Category one:

34
00:00:57,478 --> 00:00:59,298
unit, or mass.

35
00:00:59,338 --> 00:01:01,216
This kind of noun
is a unit,

36
00:01:01,256 --> 00:01:03,565
and a unit contains
many things.

37
00:01:04,203 --> 00:01:05,889
Look. A chair.

38
00:01:05,929 --> 00:01:07,459
A table. A bed.

39
00:01:07,499 --> 00:01:08,783
A sofa. A desk.

40
00:01:08,823 --> 00:01:10,265
Many individual
things.

41
00:01:10,903 --> 00:01:12,862
English groups
them together.

42
00:01:12,902 --> 00:01:14,454
It draws one
circle around

43
00:01:14,494 --> 00:01:16,101
them,
and treats the whole

44
00:01:16,141 --> 00:01:17,283
group as one unit,

45
00:01:17,323 --> 00:01:18,765
called furniture.

46
00:01:19,703 --> 00:01:21,246
Why? Because
furniture

47
00:01:21,286 --> 00:01:23,316
is not the name
of one object.

48
00:01:23,356 --> 00:01:25,576
It’s the name
of the whole collection.

49
00:01:25,616 --> 00:01:27,457
The word comes
from ‘furnish’:

50
00:01:27,497 --> 00:01:29,725
everything you
furnish a room with.

51
00:01:30,470 --> 00:01:32,217
And the word
unit means one.

52
00:01:32,257 --> 00:01:33,299
One thing.

53
00:01:33,339 --> 00:01:35,236
And we can’t
add s to one.

54
00:01:35,870 --> 00:01:37,481
One is always
singular.

55
00:01:37,521 --> 00:01:39,039
We can’t add
s to one,

56
00:01:39,079 --> 00:01:40,886
so the word
stays singular,

57
00:01:40,926 --> 00:01:42,604
and so does
the verb.

58
00:01:43,237 --> 00:01:45,196
So the correct
sentence is:

59
00:01:45,236 --> 00:01:47,403
‘The furniture
is expensive.’

60
00:01:48,537 --> 00:01:50,216
The old school
rule says:

61
00:01:50,256 --> 00:01:52,224
furniture,
luggage,

62
00:01:52,264 --> 00:01:53,099
baggage
and crockery

63
00:01:53,139 --> 00:01:54,285
are always
singular.

64
00:01:54,325 --> 00:01:55,368
Memorise it.

65
00:01:55,408 --> 00:01:57,827
Skip that.
Here’s the logic:

66
00:01:57,867 --> 00:01:59,169
many individual
things

67
00:01:59,209 --> 00:02:01,002
are named
as one unit.

68
00:02:01,042 --> 00:02:02,228
Unit means one,

69
00:02:02,268 --> 00:02:04,151
and one is always
singular.

70
00:02:04,903 --> 00:02:07,125
The same logic
works everywhere.

71
00:02:07,165 --> 00:02:07,921
Luggage:

72
00:02:07,961 --> 00:02:09,713
bags and suitcases
together,

73
00:02:09,753 --> 00:02:11,466
one whole.
Clothing:

74
00:02:11,506 --> 00:02:12,758
shirts
and trousers,

75
00:02:12,798 --> 00:02:13,924
one category.

76
00:02:13,964 --> 00:02:14,540
Crockery:

77
00:02:14,580 --> 00:02:16,845
plates and cups,
one collection.

78
00:02:17,470 --> 00:02:18,701
And if you
need a number,

79
00:02:18,741 --> 00:02:20,075
count a piece:

80
00:02:20,115 --> 00:02:21,405
a piece
of furniture,

81
00:02:21,445 --> 00:02:22,635
a piece
of luggage,

82
00:02:22,675 --> 00:02:24,396
an item
of clothing.

83
00:02:25,137 --> 00:02:26,406
Now think
about it.

84
00:02:26,446 --> 00:02:28,152
Why can you say
‘two chairs’,

85
00:02:28,192 --> 00:02:29,687
but not ‘two
rice’?

86
00:02:31,037 --> 00:02:31,858
Error two.

87
00:02:31,898 --> 00:02:33,655
‘This ring
is made of golds.’

88
00:02:33,695 --> 00:02:34,432
Wrong.

89
00:02:34,472 --> 00:02:36,499
‘This ring
is made of gold.’

90
00:02:37,237 --> 00:02:38,399
Category two:

91
00:02:38,439 --> 00:02:39,494
material.

92
00:02:39,534 --> 00:02:41,028
A material
is what something

93
00:02:41,068 --> 00:02:42,172
is made from.

94
00:02:42,212 --> 00:02:43,659
It’s a substance.

95
00:02:44,303 --> 00:02:46,442
Take gold.
It’s a substance,

96
00:02:46,482 --> 00:02:47,726
and it’s used
to make many

97
00:02:47,766 --> 00:02:48,752
different things:

98
00:02:48,792 --> 00:02:51,301
a ring, a coin,
a crown.

99
00:02:51,937 --> 00:02:53,461
We can count
the things:

100
00:02:53,501 --> 00:02:55,468
one ring,
two coins.

101
00:02:55,508 --> 00:02:56,839
But the substance
itself

102
00:02:56,879 --> 00:02:58,928
is measured,
not counted.

103
00:02:58,968 --> 00:03:00,951
We weigh gold
in grams.

104
00:03:01,703 --> 00:03:03,215
So a material
noun,

105
00:03:03,255 --> 00:03:04,605
used
as a material,

106
00:03:04,645 --> 00:03:06,152
isn’t made plural.

107
00:03:06,192 --> 00:03:08,365
‘This ring
is made of gold.’

108
00:03:09,503 --> 00:03:11,300
The old school
rule says:

109
00:03:11,340 --> 00:03:13,197
brick, sand, milk,

110
00:03:13,237 --> 00:03:15,198
glass and water
are always singular.

111
00:03:15,238 --> 00:03:16,314
Memorise it.

112
00:03:16,354 --> 00:03:18,447
Skip that.
The logic:

113
00:03:18,487 --> 00:03:20,134
it’s the material
used to make

114
00:03:20,174 --> 00:03:21,027
other things,

115
00:03:21,067 --> 00:03:22,288
so you measure
it rather than

116
00:03:22,328 --> 00:03:23,461
count it.

117
00:03:24,203 --> 00:03:26,519
Silver:
used for jewellery,

118
00:03:26,559 --> 00:03:28,077
measured,
not counted.

119
00:03:28,117 --> 00:03:29,194
Wood:
used for furniture,

120
00:03:29,234 --> 00:03:31,156
measured
in cubic metres.

121
00:03:31,196 --> 00:03:32,912
Water:
used for drinking,

122
00:03:32,952 --> 00:03:34,585
measured
in litres.

123
00:03:35,237 --> 00:03:36,829
One careful note.

124
00:03:36,869 --> 00:03:37,888
When you
mean separate

125
00:03:37,928 --> 00:03:39,562
objects,
you can count them:

126
00:03:39,602 --> 00:03:41,001
a wall made
of bricks,

127
00:03:41,041 --> 00:03:43,073
or two glasses
of water.

128
00:03:43,113 --> 00:03:44,237
But as a material,

129
00:03:44,277 --> 00:03:45,546
it’s brick
and glass,

130
00:03:45,586 --> 00:03:46,819
with no s.

131
00:03:47,570 --> 00:03:48,935
Now think
about it.

132
00:03:48,975 --> 00:03:50,236
Do we say
‘one milk’,

133
00:03:50,276 --> 00:03:52,024
or ‘one litre
of milk’?

134
00:03:53,370 --> 00:03:54,169
Error three.

135
00:03:54,209 --> 00:03:56,192
‘I ate too many
rice for lunch.’

136
00:03:56,232 --> 00:03:56,876
Wrong.

137
00:03:56,916 --> 00:03:59,072
‘I ate too much
rice for lunch.’

138
00:03:59,803 --> 00:04:01,133
Category three:

139
00:04:01,173 --> 00:04:02,104
measured.

140
00:04:02,144 --> 00:04:03,961
Food and substances
like rice

141
00:04:04,001 --> 00:04:05,618
are measured
by amount,

142
00:04:05,658 --> 00:04:08,073
not counted
as separate items.

143
00:04:08,703 --> 00:04:11,324
Rice comes as many
tiny grains.

144
00:04:11,364 --> 00:04:13,339
We don’t count
them one by one.

145
00:04:13,379 --> 00:04:15,361
We measure
the amount instead:

146
00:04:15,401 --> 00:04:17,968
a cup of rice,
a kilo of rice,

147
00:04:18,008 --> 00:04:19,469
a plate of rice.

148
00:04:20,203 --> 00:04:22,487
Measured,
not counted,

149
00:04:22,527 --> 00:04:24,320
so it always takes
a singular verb.

150
00:04:24,360 --> 00:04:26,433
‘This rice
is delicious.’

151
00:04:27,570 --> 00:04:28,996
Material and food
nouns can

152
00:04:29,036 --> 00:04:29,765
look alike,

153
00:04:29,805 --> 00:04:31,271
so here’s
the test:

154
00:04:31,311 --> 00:04:32,645
do we buy it
in a measured

155
00:04:32,685 --> 00:04:33,770
amount,
like a kilo

156
00:04:33,810 --> 00:04:36,030
or a litre,
and not in pieces?

157
00:04:36,070 --> 00:04:37,979
If yes,
it’s uncountable.

158
00:04:38,019 --> 00:04:39,883
The old school
rule says food,

159
00:04:39,923 --> 00:04:41,401
meat, sugar, salt,

160
00:04:41,441 --> 00:04:43,767
tea and bread
are always singular.

161
00:04:43,807 --> 00:04:45,436
The logic:
they’re measured,

162
00:04:45,476 --> 00:04:46,688
not counted.

163
00:04:47,437 --> 00:04:48,013
Bread:

164
00:04:48,053 --> 00:04:50,008
measured in loaves
or slices.

165
00:04:50,048 --> 00:04:52,079
Milk:
measured in litres.

166
00:04:52,119 --> 00:04:54,267
Salt:
measured by weight.

167
00:04:55,003 --> 00:04:56,406
Now think
about it.

168
00:04:56,446 --> 00:04:57,943
Can you say
‘two rice’,

169
00:04:57,983 --> 00:04:59,183
or do you need
to say ‘two

170
00:04:59,223 --> 00:05:00,729
kilos of rice’?

171
00:05:02,070 --> 00:05:02,930
Error four.

172
00:05:02,970 --> 00:05:05,117
‘He has many
knowledges about art.’

173
00:05:05,157 --> 00:05:05,984
Wrong.

174
00:05:06,024 --> 00:05:07,019
‘He has a lot
of knowledge

175
00:05:07,059 --> 00:05:08,228
about art.’

176
00:05:08,970 --> 00:05:10,127
Category four:

177
00:05:10,167 --> 00:05:11,270
abstract.

178
00:05:11,310 --> 00:05:12,704
An abstract
noun has no

179
00:05:12,744 --> 00:05:14,002
physical form.

180
00:05:14,042 --> 00:05:15,992
It can’t be
held or touched.

181
00:05:16,637 --> 00:05:18,384
It’s an idea,
a quality,

182
00:05:18,424 --> 00:05:19,531
or a state.

183
00:05:19,571 --> 00:05:20,854
Knowledge,
honesty,

184
00:05:20,894 --> 00:05:22,396
advice, happiness.

185
00:05:22,436 --> 00:05:23,594
You can’t put
them on a table

186
00:05:23,634 --> 00:05:24,955
and count them.

187
00:05:25,703 --> 00:05:27,361
You experience it,

188
00:05:27,401 --> 00:05:28,376
show it,
or possess it,

189
00:05:28,416 --> 00:05:29,916
but you never
count it.

190
00:05:29,956 --> 00:05:31,149
So it’s singular:

191
00:05:31,189 --> 00:05:33,181
‘Her knowledge
is impressive.’

192
00:05:34,303 --> 00:05:35,751
The old school
rule says

193
00:05:35,791 --> 00:05:37,348
advice,
information,

194
00:05:37,388 --> 00:05:38,583
evidence
and knowledge

195
00:05:38,623 --> 00:05:40,120
are always
singular.

196
00:05:40,160 --> 00:05:42,191
Skip that.
The logic:

197
00:05:42,231 --> 00:05:43,857
we can’t physically
hold them,

198
00:05:43,897 --> 00:05:45,411
or count them
directly.

199
00:05:45,451 --> 00:05:46,464
Need a number?

200
00:05:46,504 --> 00:05:47,643
A piece of advice,

201
00:05:47,683 --> 00:05:49,069
a piece
of information,

202
00:05:49,109 --> 00:05:50,637
a piece
of evidence.

203
00:05:51,370 --> 00:05:52,752
Now think
about it.

204
00:05:52,792 --> 00:05:54,378
Can you hold
honesty in your

205
00:05:54,418 --> 00:05:56,207
hand, or can you
only show it,

206
00:05:56,247 --> 00:05:58,512
have it,
or experience it?

207
00:05:59,837 --> 00:06:00,841
Error five.

208
00:06:00,881 --> 00:06:02,984
‘She does many
works every day.’

209
00:06:03,024 --> 00:06:03,769
Wrong.

210
00:06:03,809 --> 00:06:05,971
‘She does a lot
of work every day.’

211
00:06:06,703 --> 00:06:08,075
Category five:

212
00:06:08,115 --> 00:06:10,313
activity,
or process.

213
00:06:10,353 --> 00:06:11,781
We talk about
an ongoing

214
00:06:11,821 --> 00:06:13,114
process
as a whole,

215
00:06:13,154 --> 00:06:14,877
not as separate
actions.

216
00:06:15,503 --> 00:06:17,473
Work has many
small steps:

217
00:06:17,513 --> 00:06:19,526
emails, calls,
meetings,

218
00:06:19,566 --> 00:06:20,538
reports.

219
00:06:20,578 --> 00:06:21,922
But we talk
about it as one

220
00:06:21,962 --> 00:06:23,437
ongoing whole.

221
00:06:24,170 --> 00:06:25,201
One process,

222
00:06:25,241 --> 00:06:26,613
not separate
actions,

223
00:06:26,653 --> 00:06:28,249
so a singular
verb.

224
00:06:28,289 --> 00:06:30,256
‘Her research
is impressive.’

225
00:06:31,403 --> 00:06:33,253
The old school
rule says work,

226
00:06:33,293 --> 00:06:34,644
research,
progress,

227
00:06:34,684 --> 00:06:36,371
travel and exercise
are always

228
00:06:36,411 --> 00:06:38,440
singular.
Skip that.

229
00:06:38,480 --> 00:06:39,384
The logic:

230
00:06:39,424 --> 00:06:40,904
you’re talking
about the whole

231
00:06:40,944 --> 00:06:42,947
activity,
not separate actions.

232
00:06:42,987 --> 00:06:43,843
Research:

233
00:06:43,883 --> 00:06:45,768
the whole process
of studying.

234
00:06:45,808 --> 00:06:46,415
Travel:

235
00:06:46,455 --> 00:06:48,217
going from
place to place.

236
00:06:48,257 --> 00:06:48,942
Shopping:

237
00:06:48,982 --> 00:06:51,361
the whole activity
of buying things.

238
00:06:52,103 --> 00:06:53,418
Now think
about it.

239
00:06:53,458 --> 00:06:54,881
Do we say
‘I do a work’,

240
00:06:54,921 --> 00:06:56,365
or ‘I do work’?

241
00:06:57,703 --> 00:06:59,098
Let’s put all
the examples

242
00:06:59,138 --> 00:07:00,741
on the board.

243
00:07:01,370 --> 00:07:02,696
Unit or mass:

244
00:07:02,736 --> 00:07:04,151
furniture,
luggage,

245
00:07:04,191 --> 00:07:05,606
baggage,
equipment,

246
00:07:05,646 --> 00:07:07,061
machinery,
garbage,

247
00:07:07,101 --> 00:07:08,171
rubbish, clothing,

248
00:07:08,211 --> 00:07:09,411
stationery,
crockery,

249
00:07:09,451 --> 00:07:11,248
cutlery,
scenery and traffic.

250
00:07:11,288 --> 00:07:13,264
Many things,
one unit.

251
00:07:14,003 --> 00:07:16,297
Material: gold,
silver, iron,

252
00:07:16,337 --> 00:07:17,877
copper,
steel, wood,

253
00:07:17,917 --> 00:07:19,355
stone, sand, clay,

254
00:07:19,395 --> 00:07:20,884
cotton,
wool, silk,

255
00:07:20,924 --> 00:07:21,921
leather, plastic,

256
00:07:21,961 --> 00:07:22,894
glass and paper.

257
00:07:22,934 --> 00:07:24,649
It’s what things
are made of.

258
00:07:25,570 --> 00:07:27,399
Measured: food,
rice, bread,

259
00:07:27,439 --> 00:07:28,750
butter,
cheese, meat,

260
00:07:28,790 --> 00:07:30,017
sugar,
flour, salt,

261
00:07:30,057 --> 00:07:31,202
milk, water, tea,

262
00:07:31,242 --> 00:07:32,344
coffee and honey.

263
00:07:32,384 --> 00:07:33,912
Measured
by amount.

264
00:07:34,637 --> 00:07:37,437
Abstract: advice,
information,

265
00:07:37,477 --> 00:07:38,698
knowledge,
education,

266
00:07:38,738 --> 00:07:39,786
honesty, justice,

267
00:07:39,826 --> 00:07:40,981
patience, courage,

268
00:07:41,021 --> 00:07:42,248
wisdom, happiness,

269
00:07:42,288 --> 00:07:44,294
freedom,
peace and evidence.

270
00:07:44,334 --> 00:07:45,859
No physical form.

271
00:07:46,603 --> 00:07:48,347
Activity
or process:

272
00:07:48,387 --> 00:07:50,136
work, research,
progress,

273
00:07:50,176 --> 00:07:51,363
travel, exercise,

274
00:07:51,403 --> 00:07:52,539
training, sleep,

275
00:07:52,579 --> 00:07:54,134
shopping
and business.

276
00:07:54,174 --> 00:07:55,881
One whole
activity.

277
00:07:56,803 --> 00:07:58,789
So don’t memorise
the list.

278
00:07:58,829 --> 00:07:59,956
